  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nUpper heights will begin to rise tonight through the weekend as \nupper troughing over the eastern CONUS begins to relent, and a \nstrong upper ridge shifts east from New Mexico towards Tennessee. \nThis ridge eventually becomes centered over East Tennessee and the \nsouthern Appalachian region by Tue/Wed of next week and remains in \ncontrol of our weather through the end of the period. \n\nGenerally speaking, most areas will be dry through the end of the \nforecast period. However, despite the strengthening ridge, guidance \nis pretty insistent on daily rain chances over mainly the Smokies \neach afternoon and evening. The ridge, while strong, isn't \nexceptionally strong and there are troughs moving through southern\nCanada that keep it from amplifying too much. So it seems\nreasonable to me to expect at least some isolated convection over\nthe Smokies each day. Equally as reasonable is the lack of \nconvection in the lower elevations beneath the strong subsidence.\n\nAs for temperatures, highs warm to around 90 in the valley this \nweekend, and then firmly into the low to mid 90s from Tuesday \nonward. Chattanooga may even break into the upper 90s before it's \nall said and done. Not record high territory, but definitely hot and \nabove normal for this time of year. In addition to the warm \ntemperatures, dewpoints will also be fairly high which will result \nin heat index values of 100-105 degrees many afternoons next week.",
